WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.500
Capillary walls let water and small solutes through.

2
00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.280
So why doesn't all your plasma end up in your tissues?

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.260
Something must hold most of it in.

4
00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:19.070
The first film showed that blood flows down a pressure difference, divided by a

5
00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:25.150
resistance. Across the capillary wall, the same rule decides which way fluid moves.

6
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.330
Two pressures act across the wall.

7
00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:34.350
The first is blood pressure inside the capillary, minus the tissue fluid's pressure

8
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:37.450
outside. It pushes fluid outward.

9
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.910
Physiologists call it hydrostatic pressure.

10
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:45.783
The second comes from plasma proteins, mostly albumin.

11
00:00:46.843 --> 00:00:49.543
Few cross the wall, so they pull water back in.

12
00:00:50.763 --> 00:00:53.903
Protein outside pulls the other way, so the difference counts.

13
00:00:55.163 --> 00:00:56.883
That pull is the oncotic pressure.

14
00:00:57.983 --> 00:01:04.503
In 1896, Ernest Starling, working with dogs, argued that these two pressures set the

15
00:01:04.503 --> 00:01:05.503
direction of flow.

16
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:09.703
But neither had been measured inside a single capillary.

17
00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:15.023
In 1927, Eugene Landis took up the test in frogs.

18
00:01:16.383 --> 00:01:21.303
He spread out the mesentery, the thin sheet that holds the gut, and watched single

19
00:01:21.303 --> 00:01:22.843
capillaries under a microscope.

20
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:28.483
A fine glass pipette slid into one capillary to record its pressure.

21
00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:32.843
Then he pressed the capillary shut with a fine glass rod.

22
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:37.437
With the flow stopped, the trapped red cells drifted slowly.

23
00:01:38.757 --> 00:01:41.837
They moved toward the block when fluid was leaving through the wall.

24
00:01:43.077 --> 00:01:44.837
They moved away when fluid entered.

25
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.560
From the cells' speed and the vessel's size, he worked out the fluid crossing each

26
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:55.780
patch of wall. Then he plotted that rate against the pressure he had measured.

27
00:01:56.883 --> 00:02:01.783
If Starling was right, what should happen when capillary pressure falls below the

28
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:03.163
pull of the plasma proteins?

29
00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:06.043
Which way should the fluid go?

30
00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:14.487
Fluid should come in, and it did.

31
00:02:15.847 --> 00:02:18.747
Filtration rose roughly in proportion to pressure.

32
00:02:19.927 --> 00:02:24.327
It fell to zero near the oncotic pressure of frog plasma, then reversed.

33
00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:29.547
So across the wall, the driving pressure is the push minus the pull.

34
00:02:30.707 --> 00:02:33.467
The more the push wins, the faster fluid leaves.

35
00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:36.707
When the pull wins, fluid comes in.

36
00:02:37.717 --> 00:02:41.517
But Landis measured single capillaries in one frog tissue.

37
00:02:42.797 --> 00:02:44.737
His result ruled in the balance there.

38
00:02:45.837 --> 00:02:48.857
It did not show what every capillary does over hours.

39
00:02:49.967 --> 00:02:55.587
Later reviews concluded that a lasting return at the venous end lacks reproducible

40
00:02:55.587 --> 00:03:01.767
support. In most tissues, capillaries filter slowly, and lymph vessels carry the

41
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:02.367
fluid back.
